            <ArticleTitle>Esophageal atresia and diverticulum of Kommerell.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>303-4</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Diverticulum of Kommerell is an outpouching from the descending aorta that, along with an aberrant subclavian artery and ductus arteriosus, may form a vascular ring compressing the trachea and/or the esophagus. It has been reported as an incidental finding as well as the cause of persistent dysphagia, cough, &quot;asthma,&quot; or airway compromise at various ages but has never been reported as a cause of esophageal atresia in a newborn. We present a newborn with diverticulum of Kommerell, a vascular ring, and esophageal atresia.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
